From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Julien Etienne <julien.etienne@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 16:57:35 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 377/378] pinctrl: mediatek: mt6797: use the EINT mux mode on
 dedicated EINT pads

On MT6797, GPIO61-68 and GPIO85-93 reach EINT0-16 only in their EINTn mux
function, which is mode 1. The pin table gives every EINT-capable pin
eint_m = 0. Requesting such an interrupt makes mtk_xt_set_gpio_as_eint()
write that value into the pad mode. That switches the pad from its EINT
function back to GPIO, even when the device tree selected the EINT
function. The interrupt then never fires.

The Gemini PDA shows it on two pads. GPIO66 (EINT5, lid sensor) followed
the lid as a GPIO, but its interrupt never fired. GPIO87 (EINT10, AW9523
keyboard INT) was muxed to EINT10 by its pinctrl state. After the
interrupt was requested, it read back in mode 0 and never fired with the
expander's row interrupts enabled. The PMIC's virtual EINT176 works,
because virtual pins skip the pad write.

Set eint_m to 1 for the 17 pads whose table entry lists an EINTn
function at mode 1. The other pins keep mode 0 until their routing is
known.

Not yet run on hardware.
